## 4. Read the waterfall: an N+1
   concept: a waterfall puts every span on one time axis; a comb of identical short queries, one after another, is an N+1: one query for a list, then one per item
   1502 queries; the most repeated, 1500 times: SELECT coalesce(sum(amount), 0) AS total, count(*)::int AS months, max(period)::text AS last_period FROM contributions WHERE member_id = $1
   the API span lasts 2423 ms; its query spans add up to 2157 ms, median 0.73 ms each
   Postgres' own log says executing all 1502 took 177 ms: the rest is 1500 round trips, one after another
   wrote out/trace-before.html (waterfall) and out/trace-before.json (the 1505 spans as the collector stored them)

## 5. The fix, and the new waterfall
   concept: one query with a join and GROUP BY replaces the loop; the same traffic again, the same three signals compared
